I just bought the German version of the DN1080 on August 14th. At first glance, the quality of the exterior workmanship is extremely poor.
1. The front panel and all the knobs are made of plastic.
2. The audio input jack on the rear panel, as well as the speaker output terminals, are of very poor quality. Any CD/DVD player combo that costs between 1000 and 2000 yuan would be much better than this product.
3. The top cover is thin, and my transformer is also dented, so I had to open the box by hand to restore it, and the top cover and front panel joints loosen.
4. The center of gravity of the entire machine is biased towards the transformer side, making it very awkward to move.
It feels like a basic entry-level model from SONY, just looking at the quality of construction; it’s produced in Malaysia.
It seems that it would be much better for Sony to have manufacturing in China done by local contractors rather than by companies like Xiaomi.
5. The machine generates a lot of heat. When using it to watch a movie in 2-channel mode for about 2 hours, the temperature of the top cover reaches 40–50°C when touched by hand. I’m not sure if this is because the transformer is too large and therefore produces more heat.
These days, switch-mode power supplies are more commonly used; that huge transformer must weigh about 4 kilograms.
Sony should use passive cooling fans with silent operation inside the devices. Upon opening the box, all that was visible were large aluminum heat sinks attached to the pipes.
It's not feeling good.
First of all, the box isn’t very sturdy; it bends with just a little pressure. Secondly, the design on the back is intended to prevent the ABS material on the box’s surface from scratching when the box is placed flat, but it doesn’t really work effectively. Furthermore, the inside of the box is just covered with a piece of fabric, and the area where the handles are located is clearly visible… There are all sorts of issues with this product. Finally, the zipper operates a bit smoothly.
PS: The wheels feel really good.
Overall it’s good, but there are a few minor issues.
For my first purchase of an under-the-sink water filter, I read several reviews and felt that LAICA was more cost-effective, so I chose this one. After receiving it, I followed the instructions to install the filters correctly. I tried using it briefly, and overall, it seems satisfactory. However, when pouring water, there is sometimes a mixture between the original water from the reservoir and the treated water (the seal on the reservoir isn't very tight).
Be sure to wait until all the water has been filtered out before pouring it!
